findings.body:
The stated goal is to accept *either* the old Chrome message (`Refused to frame ... because an ancestor violates`) or the new one (`Framing ... violates`). The failure message says so too: "match one of the CSP error patterns". The code, however, requires both regexes to match the same string:

::diff{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerExecutionTests/WebSocketCompressionTests.cs" lines="107-110"}

The two patterns are mutually exclusive. One starts with `security - Refused to frame`, the other with `security - Framing`. No log line can satisfy both, so `EmbeddingServerAppInsideIframe_WithCompressionEnabled_Fails` now fails on every browser version, including the old one the previous `Assert.Matches` accepted.

::suggestion{file="src/Components/test/E2ETest/ServerExecutionTests/WebSocketCompressionTests.cs" lines="107-110"}
        Assert.True(
            ParseErrorMessageRegexOld.IsMatch(logs[0].Message) ||
            ParseErrorMessageRegexNew.IsMatch(logs[0].Message),
            $"Expected log message to match one of the CSP error patterns: {ParseErrorMessageRegexOld} or {ParseErrorMessageRegexNew}. Actual: {logs[0].Message}");

`_receiveTcs` used to be reset only after the payload was copied out. A zero-byte read just waited for data to arrive and left the completed TCS in place for the next real read. The PR moves the reset above the zero-byte early return:

::diff{file="src/SignalR/server/StackExchangeRedis/test/RedisEndToEnd.cs" lines="406-417"}

The SignalR client's `WebSocketsTransport` always does a zero-byte read and then a real read (`src/SignalR/clients/csharp/Http.Connections.Client/src/Internal/WebSocketsTransport.cs` lines 443 and 461). Here is what now happens:

1. The pump calls `SetReceiveResult(msg1)`, completing TCS1.
2. The zero-byte read awaits TCS1, swaps in TCS2 and returns without copying anything.
3. The real read awaits TCS2, and msg1's bytes are lost.

The handshake and every later frame are dropped or misaligned, so `StatefulReconnectPreservesMessageFromOtherServer` can't work. The test is already quarantined, which is likely why this would go unnoticed.

::suggestion{file="src/SignalR/server/StackExchangeRedis/test/RedisEndToEnd.cs" lines="406-417"}
        public override async Task<WebSocketReceiveResult> ReceiveAsync(ArraySegment<byte> buffer, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
        {
            var res = await _receiveTcs.Task;
            // Handle zero-byte reads
            if (buffer.Count == 0)
            {
                return res.Item1;
            }
            _receiveTcs = new(TaskCreationOptions.RunContinuationsAsynchronously);
            res.Item2.CopyTo(buffer);
            return res.Item1;
        }

This PR is labeled as a Maestro Arcade update, but it also changes test behavior. It adds quarantines, reorders dispose calls, drops `sealed` from `WebSocketWrapper` and changes its receive logic:

::diff{file="src/SignalR/server/StackExchangeRedis/test/RedisEndToEnd.cs" lines="362-362"}

Nothing derives from `WebSocketWrapper`, so removing `sealed` has no purpose. CA1852 is only a suggestion in test folders, so it won't break the build, but it is churn. Splitting these test fixes into their own PR would keep the dependency-flow history clean and get them a proper review.
